# Visual Studio 2022 (17.8+) HTTP file. Open it and click "Send request" above a request (or press Ctrl+Alt+S inside it).
# Start Mobile.Gateway (API 1, port 5090) and Mobile.DataApi (API 2, port 5091) first.
# Do NOT commit real passwords or keys in this file.
#
# THE FLOW
#   1st time : call API 1 (request 1) -> you get a sealed "config". The phone SAVES it. It holds the client's database login,
#              encrypted: the phone cannot read it.
#   from then: the phone calls ONLY API 2 and sends the saved config as  Authorization: Bearer <config>  (requests 2-4).
#              API 1 is called again only when the saved config expires (about monthly).

@api1 = http://localhost:5090
@api2 = http://localhost:5091

# The company's details (from MobileKeyManager). x-device-id is any stable id for the phone; it is registered on the first call.
@username = <company login>
@password = <company password>
@apikey = <company API key>
@deviceId = TEST1

### 0. Health check (API 1)
GET {{api1}}/health

### 1. FIRST TIME: sign in through API 1 and get the sealed config (the "config" value in the reply)
# @name provision
POST {{api1}}/gateway/v1/provision
x-username: {{username}}
x-password: {{password}}
x-api-key: {{apikey}}
x-device-id: {{deviceId}}

### 2. From now on only API 2: the masters this company's database says to read, in order (send request 1 first)
GET {{api2}}/mobile/v1/plan
Authorization: Bearer {{provision.response.body.$.config}}

### 3. Read a master from the company's own database
GET {{api2}}/mobile/v1/masters/division
Authorization: Bearer {{provision.response.body.$.config}}

### 4. Another master
GET {{api2}}/mobile/v1/masters/tea-estate
Authorization: Bearer {{provision.response.body.$.config}}

### 5. Device check
GET {{api2}}/mobile/v1/auth/device-check?username={{username}}&deviceId={{deviceId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{provision.response.body.$.config}}
x-app-user: {{username}}
